cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this repository or its results, please cite the paper below."
title: "Self-falsifying quantum spectroscopy: a transportable necessary-condition screen for quantum-computed dynamical spectra"
authors:
  - family-names: "Bonilla Vargas"
    given-names: "Nicolás"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-6155-4391"
    affiliation: "Universidad Nacional de Colombia; SRH University Heidelberg/Munich; Daita AI"
    email: "ngbonillav@unal.edu.co"
abstract: >-
  A quantum-computed dynamical spectrum is treated not as an object to be certified but as a
  falsifiable hypothesis, equipped with a transportable battery of reconstruction-independent
  necessary-condition falsifiers. Each falsifier is a static ground-state spectral moment
  m_k = int w^k A(w) dw (for example m0 = <J^2>, m1 = (1/2)<[J,[H,J]]>), estimated independently of
  the reconstruction from the same computational-basis samples and never read back off A(w), giving
  Popperian corroboration rather than proof. The screen is demonstrated across error classes and
  domains (a spurious Drude peak in the doped-Hubbard current response, sample-based-diagonalization
  subspace truncation, a cross-domain U(1) lattice-Schwinger Gauss-law check), demonstrated on real
  IBM Heron hardware data, and equipped with a closed-form Christoffel miss-distance certificate. As
  the reconstruction is a learned generative model of the device's samples, the screen doubles as a
  falsifiable-trust layer for machine-learned models of quantum-computed data; no quantum learning
  advantage is claimed.
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-08-22"
